<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- Window theme for the native Compose app. Compose draws the actual MD3 UI.

         Parent is the DARK AppCompat theme, not DayNight, on purpose - but NOT
         for the reason this comment used to give. It said "the app's Compose
         identity is fixed-dark (ui/theme/Theme.kt ignores the system setting)",
         and that is no longer true: Theme.kt has a lightColorScheme branch and
         AuraPalette ships five themes, two of them light (Day, Sand).

         The parent choice survives its own original justification. Declaring
         this window dark is what keeps HWUI force-dark OFF the light palettes
         (values-v29 pins android:forceDarkAllowed=false for the very-measured
         reason spelled out there), and a DayNight parent would still hand the
         renderer a claim about polarity that has nothing to do with which of
         the five Aura palettes the till is actually running.

         android:windowBackground is the Calm-coloured PREVIEW-window fallback,
         not a value that matches every theme - see values/colors.xml. It is
         what the framework paints before this process draws; MainActivity
         repaints the window with the restored palette's surfaceApp as soon as
         it has one. -->
    <style name="Theme.Aura" parent="Theme.AppCompat.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/window_background</item>
    </style>
</resources>
